{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And Jethro said, Blessed be YHWH.</b> Pay attention to examine this word. Know that a paʿul form never arises except from transitive active participles. If you find one on the pattern of paʿul, examine whether you find for it an active participle that is transitive — then it is truly paʿul; if not, it is an adjective. Likewise if it is on the pattern of poʿel and has no paʿul from the binyan itself and from the root, it too is an adjective and not a participle at all. Thus שמָרַה — the participle is שׁומֵר, and the paʿul is עשר שָׁמור לבעליו (Ecclesiastes 5:12). Now we find הולך, עומד, יוצא, and all of these appear as participles, because they are on the pattern of שומר. But since their root is from intransitive verbs, for one does not say from them הלוך, עמוד, יצוא — and behold, we have no paʿul — therefore he said: כי הולך הוא (Numbers 22:22) is an adjective and not a participle. We find עצום (ibid. v. 6) and it appears as paʿul, for it is on the pattern of שמור. When we investigated this root we found וירבו ויעצמו (above 1:7) — from intransitive verbs, for the transitive would come from the intensified binyan: וַיַעְצִמֵהוּ מצריו (Psalms 105:24). And we cannot say עוצם on the pattern of שומר from the sense of עצמה (Isaiah 40:29), only from another sense: ועוצם עיניו מראות ברע (ibid. 33:15). Now we find the word ברוך on the pattern of שמור. We searched all of Scripture and found nothing of this matter in the simple binyan, only from another sense: וַיִברךְ על ברכיו (II Chronicles 6:13) — also an intransitive verb, from which no paʿul comes at all. And so the word ברוך is an adjective like עצום from the simple binyan. R. Marinus said: ברוך is an adjective from the binyan with a dagesh, and the resh ought to receive a dagesh like the entire root-class, as in ברך נבות (I Kings 21:13), and it is on the pattern of כדוב שַׁכּוּל (Hosea 13:8). But he did not speak correctly, for every adjective such as שכול, גבור, and שכור does not drop its dagesh in the construct singular or in the plural, as in ויהרג זכרי גבור אפרים (II Chronicles 28:7), הוי גבורים (Isaiah 5:22). And standing against it: בוא ברוך ה' (Genesis 24:31), ברוכים אתם לה' (Psalms 115:15)." "<b>who delivered you.</b> He said this to Moses and Aaron, who were emissaries for the Name. Through them the plagues came upon Pharaoh and upon Egypt. Therefore he said first: from the hand of Egypt and from the hand of Pharaoh, and afterward blessed HaShem who delivered the people from under the hand of Egypt, in Egypt and in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {0 "The verse citation in Ecclesiastes reads לבעליו in the Hebrew text; the canonical verse number is Ecclesiastes 5:12 in Hebrew counting. Ibn Ezra's cross-reference 'above 1:7' points to Exodus 1:7."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:42.398972789Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}